ATTACK 4 — chmod-parent vs. sub-paths; syscall really fires for nobody.
============================================================
Traced scenarioPath() (settings.go:1691): every scenario file is joined
directly under sm.settingsDir with `filepath.Join(sm.settingsDir, filename)`
— a flat layout, no per-scenario subdirectories exist anywhere in this
manager. So the "does chmod on the parent block writes in existing
sub-paths" concern does not apply to this codebase; a parent-directory
chmod/read-only-mount correctly gates every write, rename, and remove of the
files living directly inside it.
Empirically confirmed the syscall actually fires for a non-root uid that
OWNS the directory (matching t.TempDir()'s real ownership, not a root-owned
directory a non-owner can't even chmod): as `nobody`, created a directory
`nobody` itself owned, chmod 0500 it, attempted `touch` inside — "Permission
denied", rc=1. Then ran the real TestHandleWhatIfRothConversion_SaveError as
nobody with -v: PASS, i.e. the handler's Save genuinely failed and returned
500 as the test expects — not a silent, unenforced no-op.

